{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"pages":{"146796945":{"pageid":146796945,"ns":6,"title":"File:A Moorish Harem(2), John Lavery.png","index":6,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":2639717,"width":1005,"height":1273,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png/960px-A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":811,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png/960px-A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":811,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/A_Moorish_Harem%282%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Moorish_Harem(2),_John_Lavery.png","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=146796945","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"A Moorish Harem, signed J Lavery, inscribed FEZ (Morocco) and dated 1907 (lower right), oil on canvas. \n<p>A Moorish Harem is evocative of Lavery\u2019s Moroccan travels, whereby he undertook a journey from Tangier to Fez on horseback.\nThis painting is considered to be a masterpiece of the painter who surprises with its anthropological realism, in fact,  according to catalogue notes, though the artist would have seen numerous harem paintings in Paris, they were \u201call lascivious fantasies with no documentary accuracy, as westerners were strictly forbidden from entering these restricted enclaves. This image is not a product of the artist\u2019s imagination but what he observed, a search for visual truth\u201d(<sup id=\"cite_ref-2\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">[</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">]</span></a></sup>).\n</p>\nUnlike other orientalist artists who painted harem scenes, Lavery is one of the rare ones to have painted what he observed and not what he imagined, providing valuable anthropological testimony on Moroccan costume at the beginning of the 20th century, and in this whirlwind of sumptuousness, we can see sumptuous Moroccan Caftans, of all colors (pink, yellow, blue...), on which, a Dfina, long transparent dress, often with sleeves flared is stored, this luxurious clothing is called <i>\"Mansouria\"</i>, invented in the 16th century, which was first a men's clothing and then became the clothing of Moroccan women. The Moroccan mint tea ritual in its authentic tradition is also illustrated.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"John Lavery (Irish, 1856\u20131941)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/png"}]},"146796310":{"pageid":146796310,"ns":6,"title":"File:A Moorish Harem (1), John Lavery.png","index":7,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4954982,"width":2403,"height":1267,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png/960px-A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":337,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png/1280px-A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png/960px-A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":337,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png/1280px-A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/A_Moorish_Harem_%281%29%2C_John_Lavery.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Moorish_Harem_(1),_John_Lavery.png","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=146796310","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"A Moorish Harem, signed J Lavery, inscribed FEZ (Morocco) and dated 1907 (lower right), oil on canvas. \n<p>A Moorish Harem is evocative of Lavery\u2019s Moroccan travels, whereby he undertook a journey from Tangier to Fez on horseback.\nThis painting is considered to be a masterpiece of the painter who surprises with its anthropological realism, in fact,  according to catalogue notes, though the artist would have seen numerous harem paintings in Paris, they were \u201call lascivious fantasies with no documentary accuracy, as westerners were strictly forbidden from entering these restricted enclaves. This image is not a product of the artist\u2019s imagination but what he observed, a search for visual truth\u201d(<sup id=\"cite_ref-2\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">[</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">]</span></a></sup>).\n</p>\nUnlike other orientalist artists who painted harem scenes, Lavery is one of the rare ones to have painted what he observed and not what he imagined, providing valuable anthropological testimony on Moroccan costume at the beginning of the 20th century, and in this whirlwind of sumptuousness, we can see sumptuous Moroccan Caftans, of all colors (pink, yellow, blue...), on which, a Dfina, long transparent dress, often with sleeves flared is stored, this luxurious clothing is called <i>\"Mansouria\"</i>, invented in the 16th century, which was first a men's clothing and then became the clothing of Moroccan women. The Moroccan mint tea ritual in its authentic tradition is also illustrated.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"John Lavery (Irish, 1856\u20131941)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/png"}]},"146621062":{"pageid":146621062,"ns":6,"title":"File:Accessoires pour th\u00e9 marocain.jpg","index":8,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":177365,"width":1123,"height":1280,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg/960px-Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":729,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg/960px-Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":729,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Accessoires_pour_th%C3%A9_marocain.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=146621062","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"03 containers that accompany Moroccan tea: a box for mint, a second for sugar and the smallest for tea... commonly called \"M'jamaa\".","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Amal_BENTOUMI&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Amal BENTOUMI (page does not exist)\">Amal BENTOUMI</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"145556792":{"pageid":145556792,"ns":6,"title":"File:Maroc - Mauresque dans son int\u00e9rieur.jpg","index":4,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":126211,"width":512,"height":338,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Maroc_-_Mauresque_dans_son_int%C3%A9rieur.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":423,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Maroc_-_Mauresque_dans_son_int%C3%A9rieur.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":423},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Maroc_-_Mauresque_dans_son_int%C3%A9rieur.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maroc_-_Mauresque_dans_son_int%C3%A9rieur.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=145556792","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Morocco - Moorish woman in her interior. The photograph shows a Moorish (Moroccan woman) during the ancestral ritual of the mint tea ceremony. Next to it, the servant is a black woman, called \"Haratine\" which is a generic term used in North Africa to designate freed slaves imported from sub-Saharan Africa. The Moorish woman, mistress of the house, is dressed in her richly embroidered Moroccan Caftan.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"N. Boumendil","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"42435529":{"pageid":42435529,"ns":6,"title":"File:Mint tea (14823416550).jpg","index":1,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":965981,"width":2498,"height":3748,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg/960px-Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":960,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg/1280px-Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg/960px-Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":960,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg/1280px-Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Mint_tea_%2814823416550%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mint_tea_(14823416550).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=42435529","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"A Moroccan waiter in Tangier, serving traditional Moroccan mint tea. The waiter wears the traditional Moroccan Jabador and the traditional red Fez.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/24956043@N00\">katiebordner</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"145883432":{"pageid":145883432,"ns":6,"title":"File:Morocco - Moorish woman in her interior.jpg","index":5,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":1240864,"width":1023,"height":1600,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg/960px-Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":1001,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3d/Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg/960px-Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":1001,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3d/Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3d/Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morocco_-_Moorish_woman_in_her_interior.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=145883432","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Morocco - Moorish woman in her interior. The photograph shows a Moorish (Moroccan woman) during the ancestral ritual of the mint tea ceremony. The Moorish woman is dressed in her richly embroidered Moroccan Caftan and wears the Chedda on her head, which differs from one city to another. We can notably see the Amazigh (Berber) tattoos on his face which differ from one tribe to another. On her feet, we can see her Moroccan slippers, called \"Cherbil\" for women, they are embroidered with gold thread. The walls of her Riad (Moroccan residence) are decorated with Moroccan Zellij.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"N. Boumendil","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"146801159":{"pageid":146801159,"ns":6,"title":"File:Sirviendo el t\u00e9 or Serving Tea, 1899, Mariano Bertuchi.jpg","index":2,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":311403,"width":1080,"height":1340,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg/960px-Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":794,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg/960px-Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":794,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea%2C_1899%2C_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sirviendo_el_t%C3%A9_or_Serving_Tea,_1899,_Mariano_Bertuchi.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=146801159","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Sirviendo el t\u00e9 or Serving Tea. Oil on canvas, signed M. Bertuchi, Tangier (Morocco), 1899.\n<p>This work by Bertuchi stands out as a witness to the splendor of Moroccan traditions. First, the servant, who appears to be a Haratine (sub-Saharan slave) judging by the dark color chosen to paint her but also by her status as a servant, is about to serve mint tea, following the ancestral Moroccan tea ritual. The servant is presented, dressed in a sumptuous blue Moroccan Caftan, cut from brocade and richly embroidered with gold thread.\nIn ancient Morocco, it was customary for the slaves of rich Moroccan merchants to also wear expensive Caftans, a piece of clothing deemed necessary to be presentable to guests, the aim was to make a good impression. In Morocco, Moroccan Caftans are embroidered according to the regional specificities specific to each city or region. Thus, the embroidery adorning this blue Moroccan Caftan, richly embroidered with gold thread with <i>Khanjar</i> embroidery which is specific to the Caftans of Northern Morocco (Tangier and Tetouan), informs us about its provenance. \n</p>\nAround her waist, she wears a belt called <i>\"Hzam nemri\"</i> made of luxurious Fez brocade. The author does not fail to highlight the specificities of the art of Moroccan craftsmanship, objects and furniture are faithfully reproduced. We can see in particular the decorative Moroccan wall element called <i>\"El Hayti\" (or Haiti/Haiti)</i> which is found in all the rich residences and Riads of Morocco and which gives Morocco a cultural specificity specific to this kingdom.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Mariano Bertuchi (espagnol, 1885 - 1955)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"27318685":{"pageid":27318685,"ns":6,"title":"File:Th\u00e9 \u00e0 la menthe 001.JPG","index":3,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":507339,"width":1600,"height":1200,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG/960px-Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":480,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG/1280px-Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG/960px-Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":480,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG/1280px-Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Th%C3%A9_%C3%A0_la_menthe_001.JPG","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=27318685","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Mint tea","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Arnaud_25\" title=\"User:Arnaud 25\">Arnaud 25</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 3.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]}}}}